;;; A string written in a rule's `:assert` stops the run — M1e2 **W18**,
;;; register row `W18/str-in-assert` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1e2.2.18 (2026-11-04) from S1e2.2.16's parked note, where it was
;;; `build/s1e2.2.16/textkey/str-in-assert.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `holds` facts, the int `1` and the atom `a`, and one rule that tags
;;; each with the string `"lbl"`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string in a rule means what it means in a fact, the symbol it names,
;;; so root derives `(tag 1 lbl)` and `(tag a lbl)`. `lbl` is a name of the
;;; program, a constant a rule's `:assert` writes (Q-M1e2.9 (a)), so the
;;; blind enumerator proposes facts about it: R is four states over
;;; `(holds lbl)` and `(tag lbl a)`, root's holding the two `tag` facts. It
;;; is the answer of this file with `"lbl"` written `lbl`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Nothing, until S1e2.2.18: every command that saturates stopped at `s`'s
;;; first firing, exit 1, because the rule compiler did not lower a string,
;;; and an opaque slot in an `:assert` has no value to store. `ein saturate
;;; FILE` ended with `TypeError: an unrecognised `:assert` slot has no value
;;; to store`, and `ein solve` and `ein oracle` printed the message alone, at
;;; `ff023413`, in a release build and a debug one. Since then the compiler
;;; lowers a string as the loader does, `lbl` is a name of the program, and
;;; every command answers the twin's R, exit 0.

(relation holds Node) (relation tag Node Node)
(holds 1) (holds a)
(rule s () :match (holds ?x) :assert (tag ?x "lbl"))
